-- The Nile runtime's contracts over schedules. The batch driver (check/laws/runtime.l's scheduled) runs one pipeline with
-- its threads taking turns by a PCT schedule (Burckhardt et al., ASPLOS 2010) that these rules generate in full: a priority
-- for each thread, and the turns at which the running thread's priority drops below every other's. A run keeps the
-- runtime's contracts when it drops no buffer with data for want of a consumer, queues no process while it runs or after it
-- was freed, returns every block to a heap by the end of the sync, and never runs out of runnable threads; and it gives the
-- output one worker gives.
-- These laws are TESTED, never PROVED: a schedule is one interleaving of very many, so passing says no contract broke on the
-- schedules drawn, as any quickcheck run does. Enumerating every schedule within two preemptions, or two delays of a round
-- robin, found none of the races these schedules find (they need a long run, then one switch at the right lock).
-- A refutation names its schedule, runs again the same way, and shrinks to earlier change points.
-- With the races put back one at a time (the funnel's wakeup, a kernel's input position, a double queue), 1000 trials
-- refute the first two, and the schedule that exposes each is kept below as a value, checked by exhaustion.
under test: scheduled, schedule, priorities, changes
-- priorities and turns alike: every number to 400, as PCT draws its change points, then a few later turns (a run takes 300
-- to 1700); a sparser set missed races these find
let numerals = [0 .. 400, 610, 987, 1597]
defaults trials = 1000, size = 4

-- PCT's shape: a priority for every thread (unlisted ones tie at 0, and a tie goes to the lowest slot, collapsing schedules),
-- and three change points (short or empty lists made most draws one thread doing all the work)
rule prio_three:   number(a), number(b), number(c)   => priorities([a, b, c])
rule prio_more:    number(p), priorities(ps)         => priorities([p | ps])
rule change_three: number(a), number(b), number(c)   => changes([a, b, c])
rule pct:          priorities(p), changes(c)         => schedule(pct(p, c))

fact sched_workers(2)
fact sched_workers(4)
fact sched_workers(8)
fact layout_kind("words")
fact layout_kind("breaks")
fact layout_kind("points")
fact layout_width("words", 12)
fact layout_width("breaks", 12)
fact layout_width("points", 5)
fact builtin_kind("pass")
fact builtin_kind("reverse")
fact builtin_kind("sort")
fact builtin_kind("dupcat")
fact builtin_kind("dupzip")

-- text-layout.nl's stages, over 245 glyphs (two buffers crossed): MakeWords, InsertLineBreaks and LayoutText (a DupZip and a chain)
law layout_contracts: all k: layout_kind(k), lw: layout_width(k, lw), w: sched_workers(w), s: schedule(s),
                          let r = scheduled(k, w, lw, rt_glyphs(245, 7), s).
    r.contracts = [0, 0, 0, 0] && r.output = layout(k, 1, lw, rt_glyphs(245, 7))

-- The schedules that expose two races fixed on 2026-11-06, kept as values so each stays fixed: with it put back, the run on
-- its schedule breaks a contract or the output (a kernel's input position: words at seed 1144; the funnel's lost wakeup:
-- words at 98, a deadlock). PROVED here covers just these schedules, which is what it says. A change to the runtime moves
-- which interleaving a seed gives, so when the scheduler or runtime changes, put each race back and find its schedule
-- anew (found again on 2026-11-07, after the claims, when the earlier seeds 307 and 143 no longer exposed anything).
-- The double queue has no schedule here: with it put back, 22500 schedules on the claiming runtime found none, so only
-- nile_Process_claim_locked's test holds it.
fact found("words", 2, 1144)
fact found("words", 2, 98)
law found_glyphs: all k: layout_kind(k), lw: layout_width(k, lw), w: sched_workers(w), seed: found(k, w, seed),
                      let r = scheduled(k, w, lw, rt_glyphs(245, 7), seed).
    r.contracts = [0, 0, 0, 0] && r.output = layout(k, 1, lw, rt_glyphs(245, 7)) by exhaustion

-- the built-ins, over 123 pairs (two buffers crossed)
law builtin_contracts: all k: builtin_kind(k), w: sched_workers(w), s: schedule(s),
                           let r = scheduled(k, w, 10, rt_pairs(123, 7), s).
    r.contracts = [0, 0, 0, 0] && r.output = run_on(k, 1, rt_pairs(123, 7))

-- a kernel that reroutes in its body, as Nile's → substitution does (Gezira's StrokeOneSide): it passes 0 .. 4999 until it
-- reads 750, then a sub-pipeline negates the rest; thirteen buffers, so its input can run past INPUT_MAX (10) while it runs
law reroute_contracts: all w: sched_workers(w), s: schedule(s),
                           let r = scheduled("reroute", w, 750, [0 .. 1499], s).
    r.contracts = [0, 0, 0, 0] && r.output = rt_run("reroute", 1, 750, [0 .. 1499])
